{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_denial-of-service-and-source-code-exposure-in-react","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"Do the newly disclosed CVE-2025-55184 and CVE-2025-55183 vulnerabilities allow remote code execution?","answer":"No, these new vulnerabilities do not allow remote code execution, and the original patch for the critical RCE vulnerability remains effective."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_denial-of-service-and-source-code-exposure-in-react","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"What CVE IDs and severities were assigned to the newly disclosed Denial of Service and Source Code Exposure vulnerabilities?","answer":"The Denial of Service vulnerabilities are CVE-2025-55184, CVE-2025-67779, and CVE-2026-23864 (High, CVSS 7.5); the Source Code Exposure vulnerability is CVE-2025-55183 (Medium, CVSS 5.3)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_denial-of-service-and-source-code-exposure-in-react","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Were the patches released in versions 19.0.3, 19.1.4, and 19.2.3 sufficient to fix the vulnerabilities?","answer":"No, those versions were incomplete patches and required a further update."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_denial-of-service-and-source-code-exposure-in-react","fact_refs":[6],"question":"To which versions were the fixes for the React Server Components vulnerabilities eventually backported?","answer":"The fixes were backported to versions 19.0.4, 19.1.5, and 19.2.4."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_denial-of-service-and-source-code-exposure-in-react","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What do React Native users in a monorepo need to update to mitigate the React Server Components security advisories?","answer":"Only the impacted packages — react-server-dom-webpack, react-server-dom-parcel, and react-server-dom-turbopack — not react or react-dom."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_denial-of-service-and-source-code-exposure-in-react","fact_refs":[8,9],"question":"What caused the January 26, 2026 CVE-2026-23864 update, and which versions are considered safe?","answer":"The original fix for the CVE-2025-55184 DoS vulnerability was incomplete, leaving earlier versions still vulnerable; versions 19.0.4, 19.1.5, and 19.2.4 are safe."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-react-foundation","statement":"The React Foundation officially launched, hosted by the Linux Foundation, in a post published February 24, 2026 by Matt Carroll.","source_span":"February 24, 2026 by Matt Carroll ... The React Foundation has officially launched, hosted by the Linux Foundation."}
